<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Contact from './lib/Contact.abide'
import contactSource from './lib/Contact.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <ul>
        <li>component state, for what you own</li>
        <li>a query library, for what you fetch</li>
        <li>a socket client, for what gets pushed to you</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Three shapes, three loading conventions, three spellings of "it broke" — for one question the
    user is asking: <strong>is it here yet?</strong></p>

    <p>abide has one answer.</p>

    <Section title="Everything is a reactive value">
        <Example
            id="one-type"
            title="`Reactive` — four producers, one way to ask"
            summary="A contact and its notes. Watch which rows say “…” on arrival, then type a note and add it."
            files={{ 'lib/Contact.abide': contactSource }}
        >
            <Contact />
        </Example>

        <p><em>4 declarations, 1 type</em> — against component state, a query library and a socket
        client, each with its own loading convention, which is what the hand-written arm has to
        keep.</p>

        <p>The card is one file. Its <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> is the handler the loaded
        row comes from, and the browser is sent a call to it in place of the block.</p>

        <p>Four ways of getting a value, one type back — every one of them is <code>reactive</code>:
        a value the scope owns, a value with a body, a body that loads, and a value that waits for
        its first write. Hold an array in one and serve it from a <code>GET</code> and it is a
        channel, state the system keeps sending to everyone connected — still a
        <code>Reactive</code> like the other three. See
        <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a>.</p>

        <p>That is not a naming convenience:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>the branch you write for "loading" works on all four</li>
            <li>a component taking a <code>Reactive&lt;User&gt;</code> does not care where the user
            came from</li>
            <li>swapping a local value for a remote one changes how it is declared and nothing
            downstream</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Reading starts the work">
        <p>No <code>useEffect</code>. No <code>load()</code>. No mount hook to put the fetch in.</p>

        <p>A value with a body runs it as soon as you reach it, and runs it again on the next read
        after something the body read changes. The page reads it because you put it in the markup.
        That is the whole subscription.</p>

        <p>The consequence worth internalizing:</p>

        <aside class="tip">
            A read of a value that has not arrived throws <code>NotLanded</code>, and the output
            keeps a hole where it was. It does not block. The document goes out; the hole fills when
            the value lands.
        </aside>

        <p>Nothing waits for it. The document goes out, the hole fills, and
        <code>{'{#if invoice.ready()}'}</code> is the branch to show in the meantime — so a slow
        value is a page that is late rather than a page that is not there.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Same name, both sides">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>server</th><th>browser</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'getInvoice({ id })'}</code></td><td>calls the function</td><td>fetches the endpoint</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>route.url</code></td><td>the URL requested</td><td>the URL in the address bar</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cookies()</code></td><td>the request's cookies</td><td>the document's cookies</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>log.info(…)</code></td><td>stdout</td><td>console, and the app's log feed</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Where a name genuinely cannot mean anything — a page reading the incoming request during
        a static build — it throws and names the component and the accessor. It does not quietly
        return nothing.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The handler serving your page already describes itself">
        <p>Address, method, argument and result schemas, description — all of it already written
        down. An agent calling your app calls what your app already does.</p>

        <p>That is why failures are strict. A refusal is a <strong>named value carrying
        data</strong>, not a status code and a sentence:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/groups/join.ts — excerpt" code={`export default POST(() => notMember({ group: 'staff' }))`} />

        <p>The caller narrows it by name and gets <code>.data</code> typed — your own template and a
        model on the far side of an HTTP request, identically.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Who abide is not for">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>A static marketing site</td><td>It renders one fine, but nothing here earns its keep if nothing changes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Gradual adoption into an existing app</td><td>The <code>page</code> / <code>layout</code> / <code>error</code> files and the <code>server</code> directories are the mechanism, not a convention. No partial mode</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Swapping the runtime</td><td>abide is Bun and web standards, deliberately, and uses Bun's APIs rather than abstracting them</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/start/your-first-page')}>First page</a> — write one and run it</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> — the
            branch you write most</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
